y:** 1) Open the audit record writer. 2) Check `agent.type.id` and `agent.instance.id` are written on every record. 3) Check the type ID can be looked up to a stored manifest (registry, manifest store, file keyed by hash). 4) Answer no if either field is missing or nothing stores manifests by that ID. Answer unknown if the lookup store is outside this repo.\n\n**Reviewer's evidence:**  — \"\"\n\n**Reviewer's note:** Records carry both ids, but release/manifest.json holds one current type id and no registry maps older ids to manifests\n\n(no evidence cited)\n\n## A05.2\n\n**Check:** An ownership record in code or config maps each agent type to a responsible team and a current operator or on-call rotation.\n\n**How to verify:** 1) Look for CODEOWNERS, `catalog-info.yaml`, `.brace/deployment.yaml`, or another ownership file. 2) Check each agent type maps to a team and to a current operator or on-call rotation. 3) Answer no if an agent type has no owner, or owners exist only in prose with no link. Answer unknown if ownership lives in an outside catalog.\n\n**Reviewer's evidence:**  — \"\"\n\n**Reviewer's note:** Owners are in README prose and env constants; no on-call mapping file in repo beyond CODEOWNERS\n\n(no evidence cited)\n\n## A05.3\n\n**Check:** A containment control can disable a single instance or a single agent type (for example revoke its principal or scale it to zero) without touching the launching user's credentials.\n\n**How to verify:** 1) Find stop and revoke controls: admin endpoints, CLIs, feature flags, scale-to-zero, per-instance token revocation. 2) Check their scope is one instance or one agent type. 3) Answer no if the only control is a global switch or revoking a shared or user credential. Answer unknown if containment is run by an outside platform.\n\n**Reviewer's evidence:**  — \"\"\n\n**Reviewer's note:** Only global and tenant halt flags exist; per-instance containment would be done by the platform outside this repo\n\n(no evidence cited)\n\n## Your answer\n\nWrite this JSON to A05.verdict.json in this folder (or put it in verdicts.json under \"A05\"):\n```json\n{\"verdict\": \"upheld | failed | unclear\",\n \"checks\": [{\"id\": \"A05.1\", \"result\": \"holds | fails | unclear\", \"evidence\": \"path:line\", \"quote\": \"exact code\", \"note\": \"why\"}],\n \"note\": \"one sentence\"}\n```\nVerdict is **failed** if any check fails, **unclear** if none fails but any is unclear, otherwise **upheld**. A failed check needs the quoted line that makes it false: `evidence` is path:line, and `quote` is the exact text of that line. To cite two places, list both refs in `evidence` (`a.py:12, b.yaml:40`) and put each line's text on its own line in `quote`. If the check fails, add `\"violates\": [\"words copied exactly from the requirement\"]`.\n","numLines":65,"startLine":1,"totalLines":65}}}
